# ★ Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings, on every platform.
#
# Windows git defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so a plain `git checkout` on this machine rewrites
# Build.sh with CRLF. The shebang then reads `#!/bin/bash\r`, bash reports
#
#     ./Build.sh: cannot execute: required file not found
#
# and — because the failure happens in the wrapper — the build still reports exit 0 while producing
# a one-line log and leaving the PREVIOUS .efi.img in place. That is a stale image that looks like a
# successful build, which is the worst possible failure on a machine where every test costs a power
# cycle. It cost two builds in one session before the artifact timestamp gave it away.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else executed rather than compiled.
*.py text eol=lf
